\name{dbarts}
\title{Discrete Bayesian Additive Regression Trees Sampler}
\alias{dbarts}
\description{
  Creates a sampler object for a given problem which fits a Bayesian Additive Regression Trees model. Internally stores state in such a way as to be mutable.
}
\usage{
dbarts(
    formula, data, test, subset, weights, offset, offset.test = offset,
    verbose = FALSE, n.samples = 800L,
    tree.prior = cgm, leaf.prior = normal,
    monotone = NULL,
    interactions = NULL,
    blocks = NULL,
    variance = NULL,
    forests = NULL,
    control = dbarts::dbartsControl(), sigest = NULL, seed = NULL,
    factors = c("categorical", "indicators"),
    family = c("auto", "gaussian", "student", "probit", "logistic", "aft",
               "multinomial", "ordinal",
               "nbinom", "hazard", "hazard.probit", "hazard.logistic"),
    na.action = dbarts::na.keepPredictors,
    sigma = NULL, node.prior = NULL, callback = NULL, \dots)}
\arguments{
  \item{formula}{
    An object of class \code{\link{formula}} following an analogous model description syntax as \code{\link{lm}}. As in \code{lm}, an \code{offset()} term is added to \code{offset} and is evaluated on \code{test} (see \code{offset.test}), and a data-dependent term such as \code{poly()}, \code{splines::ns()} or \code{scale()} is rebuilt on \code{test} and on \code{predict}'s \code{newdata} from the training values, as \code{\link{predict.lm}} rebuilds it. A fit stores these terms, and the \code{offset} expression, without an environment: a name that is a column of \code{data} is read from the new rows alone, and every other name is fixed at its value when fitting, so a fit carries no frame of the function that made it. A formula calling a function from no package, such as one defined in the session, is refused, naming it. For backwards compatibility, can also be the \code{\link{bartBT}} matrix \code{x.train}, whose integer and logical values are taken as numbers, as in a data frame, including a sparse \code{Matrix::dgCMatrix} (any other sparse \code{Matrix} class, or a \code{sparseVector} as one column, is converted to one, and a dense \code{Matrix} is taken as a plain matrix): its columns enter as ordinal predictors, sufficiently sparse columns are stored in a compact rank-bitmap layout instead of being expanded, and the predictor-mutation surface accepts both whole-matrix replacement, \code{setPredictor(x)}, and the column-granular \code{setPredictor(x, column = j)}, which replaces a sparse column whole; a replaced sparse-backed column stays sparse, storing only the replacement's nonzeros, and a replacement's sparse columns are never expanded. Per-observation replacement of a sparse-backed column, and \code{setData}, are fixed at creation. Sparse inputs are not supported by the \code{leaf.prior = linear()} and \code{gp()} leaf models (a sparse-backed test column cannot serve a designated leaf covariate either). A sparse or data-frame test set stays resident (rank-bitmap or dense per column, the same rule as training) rather than densifying at ingestion - a test set of any other sparse \code{Matrix} class, such as a \code{dgTMatrix}, as the \code{dgCMatrix} it holds - and \code{predict} and \code{getTrees(newdata = )} route its rows through the trees off that storage rather than densifying it either. A data frame may mix ordinary columns with \code{Matrix::sparseVector} or \code{dgCMatrix} columns (assign them into the frame; they do not survive \code{data.frame(...)} or \code{I()}): sparse columns behave as in an all-sparse design, while dense columns keep categorical splits and linear-leaf designation. A \code{\link{sparseFactor}} column is accepted here (the x/y, data-frame path) exactly as the other sparse columns are; a \code{formula} object accepts one too, named like any other column (including through \code{.}), since \code{dbartsData} lifts it out of \code{data} ahead of \code{model.frame} (which cannot carry it) and re-attaches it to the assembled predictor matrix afterward. See \code{\link{dbartsData}} for the full data-frame-to-predictor mapping.
  }
  \item{data}{
    An optional data frame, list, or environment containing predictors to be used with the model. For backwards compatibility, can also be the \code{\link{bartBT}} vector \code{y.train}. The response, however supplied, is checked for near-degeneracy at double precision and warns when too few distinct values remain to fit against.
  }
  \item{test}{
    An optional matrix or data frame with the same number of predictors as \code{data}, or \code{formula} in backwards compatibility mode. If column names are present, a matching algorithm is used; when both are named, \code{test}'s names must cover \code{data}'s (an error otherwise); when only one is named, columns are matched by position instead, with a warning.
  }
  \item{subset}{
    An optional vector specifying a subset of observations to be used in the fitting process.
  }
  \item{weights}{
    An optional vector of weights to be used in the fitting process. For a gaussian response, BART fits a model with observations \eqn{y \mid x \sim N(f(x), \sigma^2 / w)}{y | x ~ N(f(x), \sigma^2 / w)}, where \eqn{f(x)} is the unknown function. Binary responses differ: a \code{"probit"} model does not support a weighted likelihood (a weighted probit has no tractable latent-variable form), except that weights identically 1 are treated as absent. A \code{"probit"}, \code{"ordinal"} or \code{"nbinom"} fit does accept weights that are all 0 and 1: those name the rows in the data set rather than a precision, so they install as the sampler's active-row mask, where a 0 row leaves the likelihood but keeps its leaf occupancy, its latent and its fitted value. For \code{"nbinom"} a 0 row still counts toward the leaf prior's centre, the log of the total count over the total exposure, as a gaussian or aft fit's response transform is taken over all rows. A \code{"logistic"} model differs again: it treats them as observation counts and so requires positive integers (its Polya-Gamma latent for a count \eqn{w} is a sum of \eqn{w} unit draws, so a sweep's time grows with the total count, and a count above \eqn{10^6}{1e6} is refused). A weight of 0 is honored but adds no information while still costing computation, and is warned about unless every weight is 0 or 1 - a vector of nothing but those states which rows are in the data set rather than carrying an inert value among real weights, and is the active-row mask outright for a \code{"probit"}, \code{"ordinal"} or \code{"nbinom"} fit; the same class covers \code{weights} going unused for \code{test} - when the model is not specified as a formula, and when \code{weights} names a column \code{test} does not carry.
  }
  \item{offset}{
    An optional vector specifying an offset from 0 for the relationship between the underlying function, \eqn{f(x)}, and the response \eqn{y}. Useful for both response families, though the two apply it differently. For a gaussian response, \eqn{y = f(x) + \mathrm{offset} + \epsilon}{y = f(x) + offset + epsilon}: the offset is netted out of \eqn{y} before BART's internal range-scaling (see \sQuote{Details}), so it acts as a fixed component of the mean rather than a free parameter. For binary responses it enters the link directly: a \code{"probit"} fit assumes \eqn{P(Y = 1 \mid X = x) = \Phi(f(x) + \mathrm{offset})}{P(Y = 1 | X = x) = \Phi(f(x) + offset)}, where \eqn{\Phi} is the standard normal cumulative distribution function, and a \code{"logistic"} fit the analogous model on the logistic link.
  }
  \item{offset.test}{
    The equivalent of \code{offset} for test observations. By default the expression \code{offset} was given as is evaluated on \code{test}, as \code{\link{predict.lm}} evaluates it on new data: a scalar is reused, and a vector given for the training rows applies only to a test set of as many rows, any other length refused. Given explicitly, it is a single number or one value per row of \code{test}, any other length refused, and a bare name is looked up in \code{test} before \code{data}. A formula's \code{offset()} terms are evaluated on \code{test}, which must then be a data frame carrying their variables, and added to it.
  }
  \item{verbose}{
    A logical determining if additional output is printed to the console, including the one-line message \code{family = "auto"} prints naming the family it resolves to (a message of class \code{dbartsAutoFamilyMessage}, which \code{\link{suppressMessages}} or a calling handler can mute on its own). See \code{\link{dbartsControl}}.
  }
  \item{n.samples}{
    A non-negative integer (\code{0} accepted) setting the default number of posterior samples to be returned for each run of the sampler. Can be overriden at run-time. See \code{\link{dbartsControl}}'s \code{n.samples} item for the full contract.
  }
  \item{tree.prior}{
    An expression of the form \code{cgm} or \code{cgm(power, base, split.probs)} setting the tree prior used in fitting, or a prior object built with \code{\link{dbartsPriors}}.
  }
  \item{leaf.prior}{
    An expression of the form \code{normal} or \code{normal(k)} that sets the prior used on the leaf values, or a prior object built with \code{\link{dbartsPriors}}. \code{linear(columns, k)} instead fits each leaf with an intercept plus a linear term in the designated continuous predictor columns (character names or numeric indices into the model matrix), standardized internally, all coefficients sharing the \code{normal(k)} prior; unordered factor columns cannot be designated. \code{gp(columns, k, lengthscale, max.leaf.size)} fits each leaf with a smooth Gaussian-process function of the designated columns under a squared-exponential kernel; leaves larger than \code{max.leaf.size} fall back to constant fits. An ordered factor may be designated under either, and enters as its level codes 1, 2, and so on, which treats its levels as equally spaced, as the linear contrast \code{\link{lm}} gives an ordered factor does; levels spaced otherwise need a numeric column holding their scores. \code{\link{xbart}} accepts the same specifications through its own \code{leaf.prior} argument. See \dQuote{Response scaling} below for how \code{k} interacts with the response's internal scaling.
  }
  \item{monotone}{
    Optional per-predictor monotonicity constraints (monotone BART; Chipman, George, McCulloch, and Shively 2022). The forest is constrained so that the fitted function is monotone increasing or decreasing in each named predictor. Supply a \code{\link{monotone}} specification, \code{monotone(directions, prior = )}, or its \code{directions} alone, shorthand for the default prior, \code{"joint"}: a named vector selecting predictors by model-matrix column name, each element \code{"increasing"} or \code{1}, \code{"decreasing"} or \code{-1}, or \code{0} for unconstrained - matching is case-sensitive; an unnamed vector of length equal to the number of columns assigns directions positionally. Only numeric and ordered columns are eligible - a direction on a categorical (unordered factor) predictor is an error. A constraint forces birth/death-only tree proposals (a \code{control} naming a non-default \code{proposal.probs} is then an error) and a fixed leaf scale, \code{k = 2} unless another number is given (a \code{k} or \code{sd} hyperprior is an error); linear and Gaussian-process leaves are not supported under the constraint. \code{NULL} (the default) or an all-zero vector fits the ordinary unconstrained model. The constructor resolves by bare name inside this argument, whatever the caller has attached, and a bare name the caller has bound to a value is that value; see \code{\link{dbartsForests}}.
  }
  \item{interactions}{
    Optional interaction constraints, built with \code{\link{interactions}}: \code{interactions(max.order = 2)} caps the number of distinct predictors that may appear together on any single root-to-leaf path, \code{interactions(forbid = list(c("a", "b")))} bars named predictors from ever sharing a path, and \code{interactions(groups = list(c("a", "b"), "c"))} confines interactions to declared groups (named predictors may co-occur only with their group-mates). The three may be combined. Named or indexed predictors resolve against the model matrix at fit time, where an unknown name, an empty group, a \code{max.order} below 1, or a reference to a dropped column is an error. \code{NULL} (the default) fits the ordinary unconstrained model. The constructor resolves by bare name inside this argument, whatever the caller has attached, and a bare name the caller has bound to a value is that value; see \code{\link{dbartsForests}}.
  }
  \item{blocks}{
    Optional block-additive constraint, built with \code{\link{blocks}}: \code{blocks(groups = list(c("a", "b"), "c"))} confines each whole tree to one declared group of predictors, so the ensemble is exactly a sum of per-group functions (a functional-ANOVA / grouped-GAMI decomposition). Unlike \code{interactions(groups = )}, which is a per-path allow-list, this is a static per-tree mask, so the groups must form a total, disjoint partition of the predictors - every predictor named exactly once. \code{trees.per.group} optionally fixes how many trees each group gets; otherwise the trees are spread as evenly as possible. Validated at fit time (an unpartitioned or doubly-named predictor, or a \code{trees.per.group} that does not sum to \code{n.trees}, is an error). \code{NULL} (the default) fits the ordinary model. May be combined with \code{interactions}. See \code{\link{blocks}}. The constructor resolves by bare name inside this argument, whatever the caller has attached, and a bare name the caller has bound to a value is that value; see \code{\link{dbartsForests}}.
  }
  \item{variance}{
    Optional heteroscedastic variance forest (HBART; Pratola, Chipman, George, and McCulloch 2020). When supplied, a second ensemble models the residual variance surface \eqn{s^2(x)} as a product of scaled-inverse-chi-squared (multiplicative) leaves, so \eqn{y_i = f(x_i) + s(x_i)\epsilon_i}, coupled to the mean forest through the per-observation precision. \code{variance} is either the plain selector naming which predictors drive the variance - a one-sided formula or character/integer column selector (\code{~ x1 + x2}), \code{TRUE} for every predictor, or \code{NULL} (the default) for the ordinary homoscedastic fit - or a \code{\link{varianceForest}} object, which additionally sets the variance forest's own tree count (default \code{40}) and tree-structure prior (default the mean forest's \code{tree.prior} \code{base}/\code{power}); a \code{varianceForest} with no \code{vars} reads every predictor, matching \code{TRUE}. Gaussian and \code{"aft"} (survival) responses and constant leaves only (a \code{linear()} or \code{gp()} leaf prior, \code{\link{dbartsPriors}}, is refused by name); monotone constraints and the latent families (\code{"probit"}, \code{"logistic"}, \code{"ordinal"}, \code{"nbinom"}), which route their own precisions through the channel the variance forest divides into, are not supported. Under \code{"aft"} the model is \eqn{\log T = f(x) + s(x)\epsilon}{log T = f(x) + s(x) eps}, so the dispersion of log survival time varies with \eqn{x} and each right-censored observation's latent log-time is redrawn at its own \eqn{s(x_i)}{s(x_i)}; \code{\link{survivalProbabilities}} then divides by that surface rather than a scalar. \code{family = student()} residuals are also refused together with \code{variance} - unadjudicated (whether the variance forest's weight-channel routing composes with them) rather than unsupported by design. The per-tree leaf prior is calibrated from the residual prior's (\code{family = gaussian(sigma = )}) hyperparameters so that a constant variance surface reproduces the homoscedastic \code{sigma} posterior. The fit gains posterior draws \code{s.train}/\code{s.test} of \eqn{s(x)}, and \code{predict} attaches an \code{"s"} attribute with \eqn{s(x)} at new predictors (requires \code{keepTrees}). \eqn{s(x)} is the fit's residual scale wherever one is reported or drawn at: \code{extract(type = "loglik")} scores at \eqn{s(x_i)/\sqrt{w_i}}{s(x_i)/sqrt(w_i)}, \code{type = "ppd"} draws its noise there, and \code{\link{summary.bart}} summarizes \code{mean.s} in place of \code{sigma}; \code{extract} and \code{predict} return it as \code{type = "sigma"}. Such a fit carries no \code{sigma} or \code{first.sigma}, and the sampler's \code{getSigmas()} returns \code{NULL}: the engine holds the scalar fixed, so it carries no posterior content. The constructor resolves by bare name inside this argument, whatever the caller has attached, and a bare name the caller has bound to a value is that value; see \code{\link{dbartsForests}}.
  }
  \item{forests}{
    Optional list of \code{\link{forest}} specifications declaring the ensembles the mean is a weighted sum of, instead of the single ensemble \code{NULL} (the default) fits. Each forest carries its own tree count, structure prior, leaf scale, column restriction and \code{interactions}/\code{blocks} constraints; a forest given a \code{basis} enters the mean multiplied by the amplitudes on that basis's columns. Any number of forests may be declared, each carrying a basis of any width; the two-forest case with a two-level factor basis is the Bayesian causal forest \eqn{y = a \mu(x) + b_z \tau(x) + \epsilon}{y = a mu(x) + b_z tau(x) + eps}: a prognostic forest \eqn{\mu}{mu} over every predictor and a modulating forest \eqn{\tau}{tau} over the columns its \code{vars} allows. Every forest past the first needs a \code{basis}, the amplitudes multiplying it being what distinguish it from the first. A single declared forest carrying a basis is refused: for varying coefficients declare an intercept forest plus one basis forest per covariate, \code{forests = list(forest(), forest(basis = ~ z1), forest(basis = ~ z2))}, or use one forest with \code{linear()} leaves. Gaussian, \code{"probit"} and \code{"logistic"} responses: under a latent family the forests combine into the index rather than into the mean, on the link's own fixed scale, so every forest's \code{sd} is stated in latent standard deviations, \code{sigma} is pinned and there is no response transform to re-anchor. \code{"aft"}, \code{"ordinal"} and \code{"nbinom"} are refused at creation, each naming what it is missing. The \code{control}'s \code{proposal.probs} mixture is a property of the fit and reaches every forest of the declaration, the frozen mixture included: an all-zero mixture holds every forest's tree structures and leaves only the leaf values, the per-forest amplitudes, \code{sigma} and the family's latents moving. The result is an ordinary \code{dbartsSampler}; per-forest fits and the per-forest amplitudes are read off the sampler rather than from the run's combined \code{train} channel. The columns a basis expands to are stored on the data object, so they are subset by \code{subset} and survive a sampler's re-creation. A basis declared here has nowhere to ride when \code{formula} is already a \code{dbartsData} object: \code{dbarts()} refuses that combination by name rather than silently discarding the declaration and fitting a single-forest model - build the data object with the bases already on it, \code{dbartsData(bases = )}, or declare them through \code{\link{dbartsSpec}}, which always takes a pre-built data object and installs the declaration, replacing whatever bases it carried. Options a two-forest model does not read - \code{monotone}, \code{variance}, a DART tree prior, \code{split.probs}, a linear or Gaussian-process leaf prior, a \code{k} hyperprior or non-default \code{k}, Student-t residuals, \code{storage = "single"}, per-column cut counts, and a \code{test} set - are refused at creation rather than ignored, as is any declaration the engine cannot honour. The constructors \code{forest}, \code{interactions} and \code{blocks} resolve by bare name inside this argument, whatever the caller has attached, and a bare name the caller has bound to a value is that value; see \code{\link{dbartsForests}}.
  }
  \item{control}{
    An object inheriting from \code{dbartsControl}, created by the \code{\link{dbartsControl}} function.
  }
  \item{sigest}{
    A positive numeric estimate of the residual standard deviation supplied at creation. If \code{NULL} (the default), a linear model is used with all of the predictors to obtain one; an explicit \code{NA} is a missing value and is refused. Spelled the same way on \code{\link{bart}}, \code{\link{bartBT}} and \code{\link{xbart}}; the sampler's own \code{setSigma}, which sets the parameter rather than an estimate of it, is a different thing and keeps its own name. That estimate falls back to the marginal standard deviation of the response when the linear model's residual standard error comes out non-finite, warning as it does so (class \code{dbartsSigmaFallbackWarning}); a design with sparse-backed predictor columns skips the linear model altogether and falls back the same way (class \code{dbartsSparseSigmaFallbackWarning}, a \code{dbartsSigmaFallbackWarning}). It is the estimate a \code{chisq} residual prior's quantile is calibrated against, so it stands beside \code{family = gaussian(sigma = chisq(df, quant))} and is refused beside \code{family = gaussian(sigma = fixed(value))}, which fixes the residual scale outright and would overwrite the estimate with its square root.
  }
  \item{na.action}{
    A function that filters incomplete rows, as \code{\link{lm}} takes one. The default \code{\link{na.keepPredictors}} drops rows with a missing RESPONSE and keeps rows with missing predictors, which every split rule routes by a learned direction; \code{\link{na.omit}}, \code{\link{na.exclude}}, \code{\link{na.fail}} and \code{\link{na.pass}} keep their usual meaning, and under \code{na.pass} a missing response is an error. See \code{\link{na.keepPredictors}}.
  }
  \item{\dots}{
    Not used for new code: the channel that lets a retired argument spelling (\code{resid.prior}, which now rides \code{family} - the residual prior as \code{family = gaussian(sigma = )}, which is its one home, so writing it both ways is refused where the two disagree; see \code{\link{dbartsFamilies}}; and \code{proposal.probs}, which now rides \code{control}, built with \code{dbartsControl(proposal.probs = )}) reach a message naming its successor instead of R's own \dQuote{unused argument} error. Any other name is refused. Removed in dbarts 1.1-0.
  }
  \item{sigma}{
    The 0.9-x spelling of \code{sigest}, accepted for one release with a once-per-session warning and removed in dbarts 1.1-0. Supplying both is an error.
  }
  \item{node.prior}{
    The 0.9-x spelling of \code{leaf.prior}, accepted for one release with a once-per-session warning and removed in dbarts 1.1-0. Supplying both is an error.
  }
  \item{callback}{
    \code{NULL} (the default) or a list with two elements, \code{fn} and \code{context}, each an R external pointer (\code{externalptr}): \code{fn} the address of a compiled \code{dbarts_draw_callback} (\code{inst/include/dbarts/dbarts.h}) and \code{context} an opaque pointer handed back to it unchanged, or \code{NULL}. Only that both are external pointers is checked here - the address is dereferenced exactly as handed, so a malformed pair crashes the session with no condition to catch, once the returned sampler is actually run. \code{dbarts} itself never runs the sampler it builds, so this argument has no other effect here beyond that validation; register it again on the actual \code{\link[=dbartsSampler-class]{run}} call (or use \code{\link{bart}}, which threads a single \code{callback} through both). See \code{\link{bart}}'s \code{callback} item for the three warnings and \code{\link{dbartsControl}}'s \code{keepFits} for the storage opt-out this argument does not, by itself, set here.
  }
  \item{seed}{
    Optional integer seed for the random number generator, a convenience mirror of \code{dbartsControl(seed = )}. \code{NULL} (the default) means not given here: it leaves \code{control}'s own seed in force, seeded or not; an \code{NA} is a missing value, as in \code{\link{set.seed}}, and is refused. A single finite number overrides the seed in \code{control}. \code{\link{bart}} and \code{\link{xbart}} accept the same argument and resolve it identically. See the Reproducibility section of \link{bart}.
  }
  \item{factors}{
    How factor columns in a data frame enter the model. The default \code{"categorical"} keeps each unordered factor as a single predictor whose splits send a subset of its levels (at most 65535) down each branch, and keeps each ordered factor as a single predictor split at a threshold on its level codes, its candidate thresholds the midpoints between consecutive declared levels (at most 65534 levels, one fewer than an unordered factor, and \code{n.cuts} does not apply); level tables are retained so that test data are coded identically. A factor's level count, ordered or not, is the declared level table, not the levels the training rows happen to take, so a declared but unobserved level keeps its own bin and test or replacement data carrying it are accepted (\code{\link{droplevels}} beforehand to model only the observed levels). \code{"indicators"} expands each factor into binary indicator columns, as previous versions always did and as \code{\link{bartBT}} still does; its training levels are kept too, so a test factor is matched to them by label, as in \code{\link{lm}}, and a level with no training rows, which has no indicator column, is refused by name. Subset splits let a single split separate any grouping of a factor's levels, where indicator columns need a chain of splits to do the same, and they give a factor the prior split probability of one predictor rather than one per level.
  }
  \item{family}{
    The response model. \code{"auto"} fits gaussian models to continuous responses and probit models to those coded 0/1, as always; a \code{Surv} response is fit as \code{"aft"}, and an n x K matrix (K >= 3) of non-negative whole numbers on the matrix interface as \code{"multinomial"} (a formula with a \code{cbind} left-hand side is refused here, naming \code{bart}; a two-column matrix is refused, naming both readings, counts and (time, status)). A two-level factor, logical, or two-level character response is also detected and fit as probit. Every resolution of \code{"auto"} is reported in a one-line message naming the family fit (printed only under \code{verbose = TRUE}), while an unordered factor (or character) response with three or more levels is an error directing to \code{\link{bart}}'s \code{family = "multinomial"}. An explicit family that a factor response cannot support (e.g. \code{"gaussian"}) is also an error rather than a silent fit of the integer level codes. \code{"gaussian"} forces a continuous fit even for a 0/1 numeric response; \code{"probit"} and \code{"logistic"} require a 0/1 response and fit latent-variable models, with fits and predictions on the latent scale. \code{"logistic"} uses Polya-Gamma augmentation. Base R family objects map as \code{\link{glm}} takes them (\code{binomial} is the logit link, so \code{family = binomial} is \code{"logistic"}, not probit) and the rest are refused; see \code{\link{dbartsFamilies}}.

    \code{"aft"} fits an accelerated failure time (log-normal) survival model: the response is a \code{Surv} object (from the \pkg{survival} package) or a two-column \code{(time, status)} matrix or data frame (status 1 an event, 0 right-censored; logical status also works, factor status does not), and \code{\link{survivalProbabilities}} produces survival-probability draws. Fits and predictions are on the log-time scale (\eqn{E[\log T \mid x]}), not the time scale. A \code{Surv} response selects \code{"aft"} automatically when \code{family} is \code{"auto"}; an explicitly conflicting family (e.g. \code{"gaussian"}) with a \code{Surv} response is an error. A \code{Surv}-like object carrying no \code{type} attribute is treated as right-censored; \code{type}s other than \code{"right"} are rejected. The response may also be a \code{Surv} left-hand side on \code{formula} (e.g. \code{Surv(time, status) ~ .}); both interfaces honour \code{subset}. Case weights are not supported. See \code{weights} for how the families differ in their support for weights.

    \code{"multinomial"} fits an unordered K-category response by a softmax of \eqn{K}{K} forests, one per category, with the interleaved one-vs-rest Polya-Gamma augmentation. The response is an \eqn{n \times K}{n x K} matrix of non-negative integer counts - column \eqn{k}{k} holding category \eqn{k}'s successes, with trials \eqn{n_i = \sum_k y_{ik} \ge 0}{n_i = sum_k y_ik >= 0}, no larger than \eqn{10^6}{1e6} since each trial costs a Polya-Gamma draw per category per sweep - or, the single-trial special case, a factor, character or non-negative integer-code vector, which is one-hot expanded to exactly that matrix with every trial 1. Column names on the matrix, or the levels of a factor, label the categories. Under \code{family = "auto"} a matrix of three or more columns of non-negative whole numbers is read as this model, but a three-or-more-level factor is not - it is an error directing here, since an unordered categorical response is equally a candidate for several models. The response rides \code{\link{dbartsData}}'s \code{counts} argument, and \code{data@y} is the derived trials vector; the per-category shift is an \eqn{n \times K}{n x K} matrix \code{offset} (entering the values before the softmax), never a flat one, which the blend is invariant to and which is therefore refused. Fits and predictions are the \eqn{K}{K} category probabilities, summing to one across the second dimension. A row with no trial contributes nothing to the likelihood and still receives fitted probabilities, as \code{glm} fits a row of zero prior weight; the first such row a sampler takes in a session warns. Like probit there is no residual scale to draw, and weights are not supported: an integer case weight is already row-wise replication in the count response, and a non-integer one has no exact augmentation sampler. The sampler is an ordinary \code{\link{dbartsSampler}} whose response and per-category offsets are mutable through \code{$setCounts}, \code{$setCategoryOffset} and \code{$setCategoryTestOffset}; see \code{\link{dbartsSampler-class}} for what the softmax refuses and why. \code{monotone}, a DART tree prior, \code{split.probs}, a linear or Gaussian-process leaf prior, a \code{k} hyperprior, a named \code{prior.scale}, \code{variance} and \code{storage = "single"} are refused at creation rather than dropped, and the response uses the matrix (\code{x.train}/\code{y.train}) interface.

    \code{"ordinal"} fits an ordered categorical response by a cumulative probit: a latent \eqn{z = f(x) + \epsilon}{z = f(x) + eps}, \eqn{\epsilon \sim N(0, 1)}{eps ~ N(0, 1)}, is cut at ordered thresholds \eqn{\gamma_1 = 0 < \gamma_2 < \ldots < \gamma_{K-1}}{gamma_1 = 0 < gamma_2 < ... < gamma_{K-1}} into the \eqn{K} ordered categories, the free thresholds sampled by a marginal Metropolis update with the latents integrated out. The response should be an ordered factor (\code{\link{is.ordered}}), whose level order defines the category order; under \code{family = "auto"} an ordered factor with three or more levels is detected and fit as ordinal, reporting the choice in a one-line message (a two-level ordered factor is binary and resolves to probit). \code{family = "ordinal"} given explicitly also accepts an unordered factor or character response - with a message that the category order is taken from the level order - and a numeric response, whose ordered levels are \code{sort(unique(y))}. Like probit, the latent scale is fixed at 1, fits are on the latent scale, and weights are not supported. \code{bart} reports ordinal fits as \eqn{n \times K}{n x K} category probabilities; see \code{\link{bart}}.

    \code{"nbinom"} fits a non-negative integer (count) response by a negative-binomial model on a log link with the Polya-Gamma augmentation: the forest fits the log mean \eqn{\eta = f(x) + c + o}{eta = f(x) + c + o} and \eqn{y \sim \mathrm{NB}(\mathrm{size} = r, \mathrm{mu} = e^{\eta})}{y ~ NB(size = r, mu = exp(eta))}, so the offset enters the mean multiplicatively as a log-exposure; \eqn{c}{c}, the log of the total count over the total exposure, is a data transform that centers the leaf prior, and fits carry it. The response must be a non-negative integer no larger than \eqn{10^6}{1e6} (the shape grid's count histogram is sized from the largest count), and \code{"nbinom"} is never inferred - a count carries no unambiguous class, so it must be requested explicitly. The shape \code{r} is estimated by default (see \code{shape}); like probit, there is no residual scale, fits are on the log-mean scale, and weights are accepted only as 0 and 1, marking rows in and out of the likelihood (exposure belongs in the offset). \code{bart} reports mean counts; see \code{\link{bart}}.

    \code{"hazard"} and \code{"hazard.logistic"} fit a discrete-time survival hazard model by person-period expansion (\code{"hazard.probit"} is an accepted alias for \code{"hazard"}). The response is the same \code{Surv} object or two-column \code{(time, status)} the \code{"aft"} family takes, but each subject observed to time \eqn{t_i} is expanded into its at-risk rows (one per period \eqn{k = 1, \ldots, t_i}), each carrying the subject's covariates, an ordinal period column appended last, and the binary indicator \eqn{y_{ik} = \mathrm{status}_i \cdot 1\{k = t_i\}}; the discrete hazard is then \eqn{h(k \mid x) = g(f(x, k) + o)} for the chosen binary link \eqn{g} (probit for \code{"hazard"}, logistic for \code{"hazard.logistic"}), and the fit IS an ordinary \code{"probit"}/\code{"logistic"} fit on the expanded rows - it adds no engine code. The time grid is the sorted distinct observed times by default; \code{hazard(breaks = )} replaces it with a given number of quantile bins or with explicit interval boundaries (see \code{\link{dbartsFamilies}}). The offset is on the link scale and replicates per subject; weights replicate and follow the chosen binary family's policy. A \code{Surv} response requires the family to be requested explicitly (\code{"auto"} selects \code{"aft"}), on either interface - a \code{Surv} left-hand side on \code{formula} works too, and both interfaces honour \code{subset} (applied before the person-period expansion). Survival curves come from \code{\link{survivalProbabilities}}, which requires \code{keepTrees} unless reading a stored \code{test} set (below); \code{bart} reports the fit with its \code{$periods} grid, and its \code{$family} records the binary link. Both interfaces accept a \code{test} set, person-period-expanded on the SAME period grid as training (no event time - every subject expands to every period); \code{\link{survivalProbabilities}} then reads its hazards straight off the fit with no \code{keepTrees} needed.

    \code{"hurdle.lognormal"} fits a semicontinuous two-part (hurdle) model for a non-negative response with exact zeros: a zero-part probit fit of \eqn{z = 1\{y > 0\}}{z = 1{y > 0}} over all n observations, glued at report time to a lognormal positive-part fit - an ordinary gaussian fit of \eqn{\log y}{log y} - over the subset \eqn{\{i : y_i > 0\}}{{i : y_i > 0}}; the two parts share no parameters and are composed from two ordinary fits at independently derived seeds, so no engine code is added and a shared variable-selection prior across the parts is not available (a recorded limitation). \code{y.train} must be non-negative and finite and must carry at least one exact zero and one positive value. By default, predictions report the natural (response) scale via posterior-predictive Monte Carlo, \eqn{E[y \mid x] = P(y > 0 \mid x)\,e^{f(x) + \sigma^2 / 2}}{E[y | x] = Pr(y > 0 | x) * exp(f(x) + sigma^2 / 2)}, using the positive part's single \eqn{\sigma} per draw (the positive part is always homoscedastic); see \code{\link{bart}} for the full \code{type} options (\code{"prob"}, \code{"link"}/\code{"log"}, the bimodal \code{"ppd"}) and the refusal list (\code{weights}, \code{subset}, \code{offset}, and \code{test} are all unsupported this arc). \code{dbarts()} does not fit this family: it composes two samplers, which only \code{\link{bart}} builds, so requesting it here is an error directing to \code{bart()}.
  }
}
\details{
  \dQuote{Discrete sampler} refers to that \code{dbarts} is implemented using \link{ReferenceClasses}, so that there exists a mutable object constructed in C++ that is largely obscured from R. The \code{dbarts} function is the primary way of creating a \code{\link[=dbartsSampler-class]{dbartsSampler}}, for which a variety of methods exist.

  \subsection{Data frame ingestion}{
    Data frame input is stored columnar: no \eqn{n \times p}{n x p} predictor matrix is retained. Code that previously reached into the sampler's stored data expecting a plain matrix should call \code{\link{extract}(sampler, "predictors")} - or \code{as.matrix} on it - to obtain the numeric predictor matrix, factor columns as their integer codes. See \code{\link{dbartsData}} for the column-type mapping (numeric, factor, sparse, and \code{\link{sparseFactor}}).
  }

  \subsection{Test offset synchronization}{
    When \code{offset.test} is left at its default and that evaluates to the training \code{offset} itself (a scalar, or a vector given for as many rows), the sampler links the two: the sampler's \code{setOffset} method re-derives \code{offset.test} from each new \code{offset} it is given. Calling the sampler's \code{setTestOffset} or \code{setTestPredictorAndOffset} methods breaks this link -- \code{offset.test} is set independently from then on, and the link never re-forms, even if the two are later set to equal values.
  }

  \subsection{Response scaling}{
    Continuous responses are range-scaled internally: \code{y} (net of \code{offset}) is mapped to \eqn{[-0.5, 0.5]} by its observed minimum and maximum, the convention of the entire BART software lineage (BayesTree, BART, bartMachine), which is what lets \code{k} (see \code{leaf.prior} above) and its defaults transfer across packages and papers. The known caveat is outlier sensitivity: extreme \code{y} values stretch the range and compress the effective leaf prior for everything else; the published workaround is to log-transform or winsorize such values before fitting, or to let \code{k} adapt via the \code{chi} hyperprior (\code{\link{dbartsPriors}}). The scale is fixed when the sampler is created; the sampler's \code{setResponse} and \code{setOffset} methods only re-anchor it when called with \code{updateScale = TRUE}, intended for burn-in only, since re-anchoring mid-run makes fits across iterations no longer comparable (see \link{dbartsSampler-class}).
  }

  \subsection{Naming the leaf prior by k or sd}{
    Because the leaf prior's \code{k} is relative to the range of the response the sampler was CONSTRUCTED on, an R program that drives the sampler between sweeps -- feeding it latents, residuals, or offsets from an outer model -- inherits whatever spread its construction vector happened to imply. \code{leaf.prior = normal(sd = )} names the spread instead, on the family's scale (response units for a continuous response; see \code{\link{dbartsPriors}} for the table): the prior standard deviation of the forest total's leaf parameter, a number or an \code{invchi} law on it. The prior mean is the response transform's shift (\code{(max(y) + min(y)) / 2}, net of \code{offset}, for a continuous response; 0 for the latent-scale families). \code{k} and \code{sd} are exclusive. Unset, nothing changes.

    The named \code{sd} is the sd of the normal prior on the leaf model's own parameter. It equals the prior standard deviation of \eqn{f(x)} at every \eqn{x} for the constant leaf only; under the other leaf models the prior of \eqn{f(x)} is x-dependent and \code{sd} bounds it in a leaf-model-specific direction. Under a \code{linear} leaf prior it is a LOWER bound, attained at the standardized covariate origin, with \eqn{sd(f(x))} equal to \code{sd} times \eqn{\sqrt{1 + \|z(x)\|^2}}{sqrt(1 + ||z(x)||^2)} for \eqn{z} the internally standardized leaf covariates (a missing value maps to \eqn{z_j = 0}, and a constant column contributes 0); the prior mean is exact. Under a \code{gp} leaf prior it is an UPPER bound over \eqn{x}, attained at rows reproducing a leaf member and on leaves past \code{max.leaf.size} (which draw as constant leaves); elsewhere the prior variance is \eqn{sd^2 c(x)' C^{-1} c(x)}{sd^2 * c(x)' C^-1 c(x)} and decays to 0 as \eqn{x} leaves the leaf's data cloud, at which point every prior draw equals the prior mean exactly. Under a \code{monotone} constraint it is the sd of an unbounded leaf value (\code{\link{dbartsPriors}} states the construction) and a LOWER bound in the interior -- the realized standard deviation runs a few per cent to about 20\% above it -- and the prior mean is NOT the prior mean of \eqn{f(x)}: the constrained marginal is skew, with an x-dependent mean that tracks the constraint direction and spans several prior standard deviations along the constrained axis (see \code{monotone} above).

    A named \code{sd} is absolute. It is applied at creation and re-derived at every \code{setModel} against the response transform then in force, and the sampler restates it after every channel that re-anchors the transform (\code{setResponse} or \code{setOffset} with \code{updateScale = TRUE}, and \code{setData}), so it means the same spread throughout; \code{setLeafPrior} changes it, and the restatement then uses the latest write. A \code{k}, relative to the data, moves with the transform instead. Two-forest (\code{forests}) and multinomial models take their forests' leaf scales from their own calibration maps and refuse a named \code{sd} rather than drop it.
  }
}
\value{
  A reference object of \code{\link[=dbartsSampler-class]{dbartsSampler}}.
}
\seealso{
  \code{\link{dbartsData}}, \code{\link{extract.dbartsSampler}}, \code{\link{sparseFactor}}
}
\references{
Chipman, H.A., George, E.I., McCulloch, R.E., and Shively, T.S. (2022)
   mBART: multidimensional monotone BART.
        \emph{Bayesian Analysis}, \bold{17}(2), 515--544.

Pratola, M.T., Chipman, H.A., George, E.I., and McCulloch, R.E. (2020)
   Heteroscedastic BART via multiplicative regression trees.
        \emph{Journal of Computational and Graphical Statistics}, \bold{29}(2), 405--417.

Twala, B.E.T.H., Jones, M.C., and Hand, D.J. (2008)
   Good methods for coping with missing data in decision trees.
        \emph{Pattern Recognition Letters}, \bold{29}(7), 950--956.
}
\examples{
set.seed(8)
n <- 100L
x <- matrix(runif(n * 3L), n, 3L)
y <- 2 * x[, 1L] - x[, 2L] + rnorm(n, 0, 0.2)

control <- dbartsControl(n.chains = 1L, n.threads = 1L, n.burn = 0L,
                         n.samples = 1L, n.trees = 25L, updateState = FALSE)

## The sampler is created once and then run repeatedly, so that BART can act
## as a conditional model inside a larger Gibbs/Metropolis-Hastings sampler.
sampler <- dbarts(y ~ x, control = control)
samples <- sampler$run(numBurnIn = 50L, numSamples = 1L)
str(samples$train)

## an outer step revises the response; the sampler picks it up on the next
## run() without being rebuilt. The prior scale set at creation stays locked
## unless updateScale = TRUE is passed (burn-in only).
sampler$setResponse(y + rnorm(n, 0, 0.05))
samples <- sampler$run()

## priors are given as expressions in dbarts's own vocabulary
sampler <- dbarts(y ~ x, control = control,
                  tree.prior = cgm(power = 1.5),
                  leaf.prior = normal(k = chi(1.5, 2)),
                  family = gaussian(sigma = chisq(df = 5)))

## an additive fit that is monotone increasing in the first predictor
sampler <- dbarts(y ~ x, control = control,
                  interactions = interactions(max.order = 1L),
                  monotone = c(1, 0, 0))
samples <- sampler$run(numBurnIn = 20L, numSamples = 5L)

## a non-gaussian family: counts by negative binomial, offset a log-exposure
counts <- rnbinom(n, size = 4, mu = exp(0.5 * x[, 1L]) * 4)
sampler <- dbarts(x, counts, family = "nbinom", control = control)
samples <- sampler$run(numBurnIn = 20L, numSamples = 5L)
}
